# Closed-cavity dissipative first-order class, all normal-zero magnetic data and topology zeros.
d='pthm-em-uniform-ohmic-modal-decay'
s=r'''Assume the constant isotropic inertial-rest material branch of [[post-em-material-closure]] with $\varepsilon,\mu>0$, $\sigma\ge0$, $D=\varepsilon E$, $H=B/\mu$, $J_{\rm ext}=0$, and put $v^2=(\varepsilon\mu)^{-1}$ and $\gamma=\sigma/\varepsilon$. The smooth equations of [[pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell]] with initially zero volume charge give $E_t=v^2\operatorname{curl}B-\gamma E$, $B_t=-\operatorname{curl}E$ and zero divergences; in the finite-energy classes below these first-order equations with the declared PEC weak/variational boundary realization are adopted explicitly, rather than deduced from a smooth-only postulate. The quadratic stored energy is $\mathcal U(t)=\tfrac12(\varepsilon\|E(t)\|_2^2+\mu^{-1}\|B(t)\|_2^2)$, consistent with [[pthm-em-restricted-material-energy]].
(a) [Rectangular cavity] In the exact zero-normal-$B$ reflection-parity and divergence class of [[lem-em-rectangular-pec-energy-completeness]] and [[def-em-guide-cavity-geometry]](c), every initial pair has a unique $C(\mathbb R;L^2)$ distributional solution of this damped system in the same class. Finite Fourier mode sums converge in energy norm uniformly on compact time intervals and are dense at every time. The Fourier coefficient equations at $k_j=\pi n_j/L_j$ are $\widehat E_t=iv^2 k\times\widehat B-\gamma\widehat E$, $\widehat B_t=-ik\times\widehat E$, with both transverse constraints and the original parities retained.
(b) [Smooth closed PEC cavity] Assume the full Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) inherited from the smooth spectral and elliptic suppliers. On a bounded smooth cavity $\Omega$, let $X,V,H$ and its smooth orthonormal eigenfields $u_j$, $\lambda_j\ge0$, be exactly [[lem-em-smooth-pec-cavity-spectral-closure]] and [[thm-em-waveguide-spectral-completeness]]. Define the full weak normal-zero divergence-free magnetic space $$M:=\{B\in L^2(\Omega;\mathbb R^3):\int_\Omega B\cdot\nabla\psi=0\text{ for every smooth scalar }\psi\text{ on }\overline\Omega\}.$$ This is the distributional divergence constraint together with weak zero normal flux; for smooth fields it is $\operatorname{div}B=0$, $n\cdot B=0$. For every positive eigenvalue put $w_j=\operatorname{curl}u_j/\sqrt{\lambda_j}$ and put $K_M=\{B\in M:\operatorname{curl}B=0\text{ distributionally}\}$. Then $$M=\overline{\operatorname{span}\{w_j:\lambda_j>0\}}^{L^2}\mathbin{\oplus}K_M.$$ The declared first-order PEC modal energy class is $H\times M$, with weak modal equations $a_j'=v^2\sqrt{\lambda_j}b_j-\gamma a_j$, $b_j'=-\sqrt{\lambda_j}a_j$ for positive modes, $a_j'=-\gamma a_j$ for electric zero modes, and $(B,K_M)'=0$ for every kernel test. Equivalently these are the curl-adjoint variational first-order equations on the finite spectral test fields and their graph-norm completion. For every initial pair in $H\times M$ there is a unique $C(\mathbb R;H\times M)$ solution of that declared realization, which also satisfies the displayed evolution equations distributionally in the interior. Finite electric and paired-magnetic modal sums, with the exact static magnetic kernel component retained, converge uniformly on compact time intervals in energy norm. No equality with every maximal rough tangential-trace realization is asserted.
(c) [Exact energy balance, decay and zero sectors] In both declared classes, for every $t_0<t_1$, $$\mathcal U(t_1)+\sigma\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\|E(t)\|_2^2\,dt=\mathcal U(t_0),\qquad \mathcal U'(t)=-\sigma\|E(t)\|_2^2\le0.$$ For positive smooth-cavity modes $a_j''+\gamma a_j'+v^2\lambda_ja_j=0$. If $\Delta_j=\gamma^2/4-v^2\lambda_j$ is positive, the two roots are $-\gamma/2\pm\sqrt{\Delta_j}$; if negative they are $-\gamma/2\pm i\sqrt{-\Delta_j}$; at zero include $(A+Bt)e^{-\gamma t/2}$. For $\sigma>0$ every positive mode decays, electric zero modes obey $a_j(t)=a_j(0)e^{-\gamma t}$, and magnetic kernel data stay constant. In the smooth class the solution therefore tends in energy norm as $t\to+\infty$ to $(0,P_{K_M}B(0))$. For $\sigma=0$ energy is constant, electric zero modes are static and the original lossless branch is recovered. The zero sectors are retained, not divided by their eigenvalues; no universal decay to zero in the presence of magnetic kernel data is claimed.
(d) [Associated second-order branch] Independently of first-order compatibility, the same spectral basis gives the damped associated electric wave for every $q_0\in V$, $q_1\in H$, with $q\in C(\mathbb R;V)$, $q'\in C(\mathbb R;H)$ and modal convergence uniformly on compact times. Its coefficients satisfy $a_j''+\gamma a_j'+v^2\lambda_ja_j=0$ with arbitrary prescribed initial coefficients. At $\lambda_j=0$ they are $\alpha_j+\beta_j(1-e^{-\gamma t})/\gamma$ when $\gamma>0$, and $\alpha_j+t\beta_j$ when $\gamma=0$. Such arbitrary second-order data need not arise from the first-order Maxwell system: its zero modes additionally satisfy $a_j'=-\gamma a_j$. This broader wave is retained alongside the physical first-order branch. All square roots and real derivatives use [[thm-of-square-roots]], [[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]] and the real-component exponential formula [[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]].
(e) [Scope] The conductor occupies the declared cavity interior while its stationary walls obey the independent ideal PEC boundary model. No arbitrary loss tensor, memory law, changing interface, open-end, rough-wall or empirical conclusion is made. Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) suffices for the rectangular/Fourier branch; the smooth branch retains its actual full Choice premise. Weak energy balance is proved by modal limits and does not assert an unprovided nonlinear weak local Poynting theorem.'''
p=r'''For (a), use the exact G2 product Fourier representation and parity constraints. Add the scalar term $-\gamma\widehat E$ to its finite real/imaginary matrix system. It is globally Lipschitz, so [[cor-globally-lipschitz-odes-have-global-solutions]] supplies a unique solution on all real time. Direct conjugate triple-product cancellation gives $(\varepsilon|\widehat E|^2+\mu^{-1}|\widehat B|^2)'=-2\sigma|\widehat E|^2$. Divergence constraints are preserved ($k\cdot\widehat E$ obeys $d'=-\gamma d$, $k\cdot\widehat B$ is constant), and damping preserves each original parity. Modal energy is nonincreasing forward and at most $e^{2\gamma T}$ times its initial value on $[-T,T]$, because its derivative is bounded below by $-2\gamma$ times itself; multiply by $e^{2\gamma t}$ and differentiate to prove that bound. Parseval therefore controls every omitted tail uniformly on compact times. Finite solutions converge in $C_tL^2$ and pass the linear distributional equations against compact-time smooth periodic tests by Cauchy-Schwarz, exactly as in G2. The current G2 continuous-coefficient-minus-FTC-primitive argument, using [[thm-ftc-first-part]], [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]], [[thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes]] and [[thm-a-distribution-with-zero-derivatives-on-a-connected-open-set-is-constant]], converts weak coefficient equations to classical ODEs and proves uniqueness.
For (b), G3's full-$X$ identity gives $(\operatorname{curl}u_j,\operatorname{curl}u_l)=\lambda_j\delta_{jl}$, so the $w_j$ are orthonormal. Smooth tangential-zero $u_j$ imply $w_j\in M$: ordinary curl integration by parts against $\nabla\psi$ has zero boundary term and zero interior curl. This is the componentwise first Green identity [[cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]] (or its direct coordinate/divergence expansion). G3's weighted $V$ completeness proves that the closed span of these $w_j$ is the closure of $\operatorname{curl}V$. Since $M$ is a closed subspace, orthogonal projection [[thm-orthogonal-decomposition-by-a-closed-subspace]] decomposes any $B\in M$ into that closure and its orthogonal complement. To identify the latter with $K_M$, take a compactly supported smooth vector test $\phi$. The actual smooth Dirichlet solver [[thm-em-elliptic-boundary-existence]] with coefficient identity, zero trace and source $-\operatorname{div}\phi$ supplies $f\in C^\infty(\overline\Omega)$ with $\Delta f=\operatorname{div}\phi$. Then $\phi-\nabla f\in V$ (zero divergence and tangential trace), and it has the same curl as $\phi$. Orthogonality to $\operatorname{curl}V$ therefore gives $\langle B,\operatorname{curl}\phi\rangle=0$, which is exactly distributional $\operatorname{curl}B=0$. Conversely if $B\in M$ is distributionally curl-free, pairing with each $u_j$ is legitimate by the actual tangential-zero collar cutoff: $\eta_\delta u_j\to u_j$ and their curls converge in $L^2$, since $n\times u_j=O(s)$ on a layer $s\le2\delta$. The distributional compact-test pairing then gives $(B,w_j)=0$. The collar exists by [[thm-euclidean-tubular-neighbourhood-theorem]] and uses [[def-the-standard-smooth-step-function]], with their stated choice premises. This proves the full decomposition, including arbitrary magnetic kernel data, without assuming a missing Hodge theorem or imposing electric tangential-zero data on $B$.
Each paired modal system and every electric zero equation has unique global solutions; keep the given $K_M$ component exactly. Orthogonality and the same scalar weighted-energy identity prove compact-time uniform energy-tail convergence for arbitrary $H\times M$ data by [[thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion]]. Finite paired fields satisfy the interior equations because $\operatorname{curl}w_j=\sqrt{\lambda_j}u_j$ distributionally by G3 and $\operatorname{curl}u_j=\sqrt{\lambda_j}w_j$, while kernel fields have zero curl. Linear compact-test pairings pass to the limit in $L^2$. The weak modal boundary realization explicitly tests these spectral equations, so the scalar ODE uniqueness and complete decompositions give uniqueness in the stated class; merely satisfying interior equations without this boundary realization is not asserted sufficient. For strong first-order solutions in the precise graph classes of the existing guide theorem, the same collar pairing gives $a_j'=v^2(B,\operatorname{curl}u_j)-\gamma a_j$. Differentiating using $B'=-\operatorname{curl}E$ and the full-$X$ identity gives $a_j''+\gamma a_j'+v^2\lambda_ja_j=0$, with $a_j'=-\gamma a_j$ at zero eigenvalues.
For (c), sum the finite identities and integrate them on $[t_0,t_1]$. Uniform energy-norm convergence on that compact interval passes both energy and the continuous $\|E(t)\|_2^2$ integral to the limit, proving the exact balance. FTC then yields its derivative for every time. This argument proves rough finite-energy dissipation without using a smooth-only local balance; the smooth reading agrees with the restricted material-energy supplier. The characteristic roots follow by completing the square; their explicit real exponential or sine/cosine solutions are verified by the stated derivative suppliers. Repeated roots retain $te^{-\gamma t/2}$. For $\gamma>0$, $\lambda>0$ the real roots are negative or the complex roots have negative real part, so each positive paired mode tends to zero; electric zero modes decay and the magnetic kernel is static. Forward contractivity plus a finite-head/finite-tail argument passes this to the whole energy norm. No magnetic kernel component is discarded.
For (d), repeat G3's weighted $V$ Parseval argument for arbitrary initial displacement/velocity. For every positive mode $Q_j=|a_j'|^2+v^2\lambda_j|a_j|^2$ satisfies $Q_j'=-2\gamma|a_j'|^2$; the positive eigenvalues have a positive lower bound, so compact-time exponential bounds on $Q_j$ control the $V$ displacement and $H$ velocity tails. The finitely many zero modes have the separately displayed solutions. Uniform limits and the same finite-sum FTC estimate used by G3 identify the actual $H$ derivative and pass the variational second-order equation against compact-time tests. This mathematical wave-energy estimate is distinct from the physical first-order stored energy; no compatibility of arbitrary $V\times H$ data is inferred. The first-order zero relation follows directly from its modal equation. No original spectral/variational supplier Statement is changed.'''
oh=new(d,'Constant-Ohmic PEC modal decay and exact energy-class completeness',s,p,['def-axiom-of-choice','lem-em-rectangular-pec-energy-completeness','lem-em-smooth-pec-cavity-spectral-closure','thm-em-waveguide-spectral-completeness','thm-em-elliptic-boundary-existence','thm-orthogonal-decomposition-by-a-closed-subspace','thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion','cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain','thm-euclidean-tubular-neighbourhood-theorem','def-the-standard-smooth-step-function'])
# Mixed discrete/continuous transform on a precise rectangular infinite guide.
d='pthm-em-open-rectangular-guide-completeness'
s=r'''Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) and the constant isotropic material branch of [[post-em-material-closure]] with $\varepsilon,\mu>0$, $\sigma\ge0$, $J_{\rm ext}=0$ and initially zero volume charge. Put $v^2=(\varepsilon\mu)^{-1}$, $\gamma=\sigma/\varepsilon$. On the infinite straight rectangular guide $Q=(0,a)\times(0,b)\times\mathbb R$ of [[def-em-guide-cavity-geometry]], declare the finite-energy reflection class as follows. Extend in the two transverse variables to $T_{ab}^2\times\mathbb R$, $T_{ab}^2=\mathbb R/(2a\mathbb Z)\times\mathbb R/(2b\mathbb Z)$. The electric components have transverse parities even/odd, odd/even, odd/odd respectively; magnetic components have odd/even, even/odd, even/even respectively. Require the extended real fields to be $L^2$ and have both divergences zero distributionally. For smooth fields this gives the four-wall conditions $n\times E=0$, $n\cdot B=0$; it is a precise weak PEC realization, not an assertion about every rough trace class. There is no end cap and no junction or open-end scattering datum.
(a) [Complete mixed transform] Let $\phi_{mn}(x_t)=(4ab)^{-1/2}e^{i\pi(mx_1/a+nx_2/b)}$, $(m,n)\in\mathbb Z^2$, be the orthonormal transverse characters. For each component first form $F_{mn}(z)=\int_{T_{ab}^2}F(x_t,z)\overline{\phi_{mn}(x_t)}\,dx_t$, then apply the exact negative-sign $2\pi$ Fourier transform $\mathcal F_2$ of [[thm-plancherel]] in $z$. Write $\widehat F_{mn}(\xi)=\mathcal F_2F_{mn}(\xi)$ and $\beta=2\pi\xi$. The transform is a unitary representation of $L^2(T_{ab}^2\times\mathbb R)$ by $\ell^2(\mathbb Z^2;L^2(\mathbb R))$, with inverse in $L^2$ from [[thm-l-two-fourier-inversion]], and $$\|F\|_{L^2(Q)}^2=\tfrac14\sum_{m,n}\int_{\mathbb R}|\widehat F_{mn}(\xi)|^2\,d\xi$$ for these reflection fields. The parity and reality relations are retained, including $\widehat F_{-m,-n}(-\xi)=\overline{\widehat F_{mn}(\xi)}$. With $K=(\pi m/a,\pi n/b,2\pi\xi)$, the divergence constraints become $K\cdot\widehat E=K\cdot\widehat B=0$ for almost every $\xi$. This is a complete transverse sin/cos and longitudinal $L^2$ Fourier representation, not pointwise Fourier convergence or a finite-energy monochromatic plane wave.
(b) [Unique energy evolution] Adopt in that weak reflection class the constant-Ohmic first-order material evolution $E_t=v^2\operatorname{curl}B-\gamma E$, $B_t=-\operatorname{curl}E$, distributionally, consistent with the smooth [[pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell]] and [[pthm-em-restricted-material-energy]] branches. For every initial pair in the class there is a unique $C(\mathbb R;L^2(Q))$ solution in the same class. Its mixed coefficients solve, for every transverse index and almost every $\xi$, $$\partial_t\widehat E=iv^2K\times\widehat B-\gamma\widehat E,\qquad\partial_t\widehat B=-iK\times\widehat E.$$ Finite transverse sums and bounded longitudinal frequency truncations reconstruct the solution in energy norm uniformly on compact time intervals; such reconstructed fields are dense in the class at every time. They retain the divergence constraints and the original parities, including zero coefficient cases without dividing by $K$ or $|K|$.
(c) [Energy and dispersion] The total stored energy of this whole infinite guide is finite and equals $$\mathcal U(t)=\tfrac18\sum_{m,n}\int_{\mathbb R}(\varepsilon|\widehat E|^2+\mu^{-1}|\widehat B|^2)\,d\xi.$$ For every $t_0<t_1$, $\mathcal U(t_1)+\sigma\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\|E(t)\|_{L^2(Q)}^2dt=\mathcal U(t_0)$, and $\mathcal U'(t)=-\sigma\|E(t)\|_2^2$. At $\sigma=0$ energy is conserved and each constrained electric coefficient satisfies $\widehat E''+v^2|K|^2\widehat E=0$, so $\omega^2=v^2[(\pi m/a)^2+(\pi n/b)^2+\beta^2]$. These are the exact finite transverse-mode matrices of the approved K10 branch; individual separated TE/TM candidate fields still have their own cutoff and zero-profile restrictions, and no TE$_{00}$ mode is created by a scalar constant coefficient. For $\sigma>0$ the electric equation is $\widehat E''+\gamma\widehat E'+v^2|K|^2\widehat E=0$ with the distinct/repeated-root alternatives of [[pthm-em-uniform-ohmic-modal-decay]](c). No signal-speed or empirical inference is made.
(d) [Scope] This theorem supplies the previously missing complete longitudinal $L^2$ expansion of the declared straight rectangular guide, including its lossless and constant-Ohmic energy evolutions. It makes no completeness claim for arbitrary non-rectangular, dispersive, nonlocal, nonlinear, rough-wall or open-end/junction problems. The weak energy result is proved by linear mixed-transform evolution and modal limits, not by applying a nonlinear smooth Poynting identity to rough data.'''
p=r'''For (a), the two transverse character factors are the complete systems used by the G2 proof [[lem-em-rectangular-pec-energy-completeness]]; equivalently use the exact sine/cosine product completeness [[lem-em-rectangular-transverse-mode-completeness]]. The normalized characters have norm1 on the $4ab$-area torus fundamental rectangle by elementary exponential integration. Product completeness and Hilbert Parseval [[thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion]], followed by Fubini/Tonelli ([[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]], [[thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]), give for almost every $z$ the transverse expansion and the integrated sum of squared coefficient norms. Apply the actual AComega unitary [[thm-plancherel]] separately to each of the countably many scalar coefficient functions; surjectivity and [[thm-l-two-fourier-inversion]] give the inverse coefficient functions. Their transverse series converges in product $L^2$, proving both directions of the isometry without an unprovided vector-valued Fourier theorem. Reflection multiplies the physical $Q$ norm squared by4, giving the exact $1/4$ factor. Parity relations follow by the coordinate changes on the finite torus; conjugation/reflection reality follows first for real integrable compact longitudinal data by the defining integral, then by $L^2$ approximation and transform continuity. No pointwise reconstruction is asserted.
The derivative correspondence is justified on smooth periodic transverse tests times longitudinal Schwartz tests by the exact identity $\mathcal F(\partial_z\psi)=2\pi i\xi\widehat\psi$ of [[thm-fourier-transform-maps-schwartz-space-continuously-to-itself]], finite transverse integration by parts and complex bilinear/componentwise pairing. The $L^2$ pairing extends this test identity to distributional divergence constraints. Conversely if $K\cdot\widehat F=0$ almost everywhere, testing with any finite transverse/Schwartz product makes the weak divergence pairing zero. General compact smooth tests are reached by the transverse $L^2$ expansions of their derivatives and the Schwartz transform isomorphism; all required pairings are bounded by Cauchy-Schwarz. Thus the exact original constrained class corresponds to the stated a.e. coefficient constraints, not just a scalar basis without Gauss equations.
For (b), fix $(m,n,\xi)$; the real and imaginary parts give a finite globally Lipschitz matrix system on all real time, so [[cor-globally-lipschitz-odes-have-global-solutions]] gives its unique solution. The entries depend continuously on $\xi$, and [[thm-continuous-dependence-of-odes-on-initial-data-and-parameters]] gives a continuous fundamental solution matrix on every compact parameter/time set: apply its local common-cylinder conclusion and finite gluing along a compact time interval, using the uniform energy bound below to keep nearby solutions in a bounded state cylinder. Multiplying that continuous matrix by the measurable initial coefficient vector therefore gives measurable coefficient functions; no uncountable choice of solutions is made. Direct conjugate cross-product cancellation yields $$q'= -2\sigma|\widehat E|^2,\qquad q=\varepsilon|\widehat E|^2+\mu^{-1}|\widehat B|^2,$$ hence $q(t)\le e^{2\gamma T}q(0)$ for $|t|\le T$ and forward contractivity. The constraints and parity/reality relations persist by the same algebra and matrix uniqueness. Spectral cutoffs $|m|,|n|\le N$, $|\xi|\le N$ preserve every relation. Their initial energy tails tend to0 by Tonelli and [[thm-dominated-convergence]], and the uniform bound makes the time-dependent tails tend to0 uniformly on compact times. Reconstruct by the proved mixed inverse: each truncated solution is strongly differentiable in $L^2$ because $K$ is bounded on that cutoff. Its linear equations follow from the test derivative identity. Compact-time $L^2$ limits pass the equations against compact smooth tests and are continuous in time with the prescribed initial data.
For uniqueness, take mixed coefficients of any weak $C_tL^2$ reflection solution and project to the same bounded spectral cutoff. The test derivative identity turns its evolution into $f'=A_Nf$ distributionally in time in a Hilbert space, where the multiplication matrix $A_N$ is bounded. Subtract the continuous Hilbert-valued primitive $\int_0^tA_Nf(s)ds$; pairing with every fixed $L^2$ vector and applying the scalar zero-distribution-derivative theorem [[thm-a-distribution-with-zero-derivatives-on-a-connected-open-set-is-constant]] shows that the difference is its constant initial value. The Hilbert primitive is the uniform limit of finite Riemann sums (continuous functions on compact times are uniformly continuous in the norm); its derivative is identified by the usual average-increment norm estimate, the vector version of the [[thm-ftc-first-part]] argument. The difference of two such solutions with zero data satisfies $d(t)=\int_0^t A_Nd(s)ds$; on an interval of length $h$ with $h\|A_N\|<1$ the supremum norm forces $d=0$. Finitely iterate these intervals on any compact time interval, in either direction. Let the cutoffs exhaust the representation to obtain uniqueness of the whole field. This proves the continuum-frequency weak uniqueness rather than assuming that an arbitrary weak solution has differentiable pointwise frequency trajectories.
For (c), finite/cutoff modal energy identities integrate over $\xi$ and sum the finite transverse indices; dominated convergence with the compact-time tail bound passes their integrated balance to the limit. The result is the physical $Q$ energy since the squared norm of the reflection extension is4 times that on $Q$. Its right-hand dissipation norm is continuous in time, so ordinary FTC proves the stated derivative. On the constrained coefficient subspace, differentiating the finite ODE once and using $K\times(K\times e)=K(K\cdot e)-|K|^2e$ gives the displayed scalar electric ODE, including zero $K$. The derivative and characteristic-root calculations are the same real component algebra as the declared constant-Ohmic modal theorem. The normalized Fourier frequency is $\xi$, so the actual physical longitudinal wavenumber is $\beta=2\pi\xi$, never silently $\xi$. The transverse parities and vector constraints remain in force; a scalar $m=n=0$ character is not by itself a nonzero TE reconstruction. The smooth material interpretation and weak evolution adoption remain separate, with no constitutive conclusion beyond the declared branch.'''
op=new(d,'Complete longitudinal Fourier energy evolution in a rectangular PEC guide',s,p,['pthm-em-uniform-ohmic-modal-decay','lem-em-rectangular-pec-energy-completeness','lem-em-rectangular-transverse-mode-completeness','thm-hilbert-space-fourier-expansion','thm-plancherel','thm-l-two-fourier-inversion','thm-fourier-transform-maps-schwartz-space-continuously-to-itself','thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces','thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces','thm-continuous-dependence-of-odes-on-initial-data-and-parameters'])
